 | D14 | ~~Privacy vs compliance enforcement boundary~~ **DECIDED 2026-09-16: gate the boundary, not the interior.** A shielded pool (if one ships — see the privacy strategy, still pre-decision) is entered and exited by ordinary EVM transactions with visible addresses, so `x/compliance`'s ante decorator already covers shield and unshield: a frozen address cannot sign a `shield()` and cannot be the `to` of an unshield. Transfers *inside* the pool stay private and are **not** enforceable by the chain. **Accepted trade-off:** an address shielded before being frozen can unshield to a fresh unlisted address afterward; the leak is bounded to funds already shielded at freeze time, not eliminated. The shielded pool is **not** allowlist-gated — this preserves D6's decided semantics (the chain never requires allowlisting to transact; only blocklisting stops a tx). | privacy track (P0-A onward), whitepaper | No code change: this is the behaviour `x/compliance` already has, recorded so it is a deliberate choice rather than an accident. The `isVerified`/`isFrozen` precompile at `0x…0900` stays available for contracts that want to enforce more strictly than the chain does — a shield contract *may* call it, but the protocol does not require it. **Amended 2026-09-17, confirmed by observation 2026-09-21** (§10.1): a shielded pool may carry its own second enforcement layer. Privacy Pools proves membership of the depositor in an on-chain *association set* inside the withdrawal circuit, so an excluded deposit cannot produce a valid proof at all — enforcement the ante decorator cannot reach, and stronger than D14 assumed: excluding a frozen depositor's label makes those funds unwithdrawable regardless of recipient, narrowing the accepted leak to the window before the next root is published. The cost is a second highest-value key (the set's publisher), an ongoing curation function, reduced privacy against the set operator, and a liveness dependency — if root publication stops, withdrawals stop. Who holds that key is an open supervisor question, not settled here. Revisit if a shielded pool moves past feasibility (privacy strategy P2) or if legal review of D6 asks for a stricter perimeter. |
+| D15 | ~~Does privacy ship, and how?~~ **DECIDED 2026-09-28: privacy is an optional feature, and Privacy Pools is integrated rather than rebuilt.** (a) **Optional, not defining** — the public path stays first-class (threat-model P6); Konstellation is a chain that offers privacy, not a privacy chain. This is the reading consistent with D6's chain-wide compliance and D7's permissioned launch, and it keeps D14's accepted leak defensible in public. (b) **Integrate, never build** — `0xbow-io/privacy-pools-core` (Apache-2.0, four published audits), proven working on Konstellation end to end by the P0-A spike (`privacy-lab/reports/000`). Writing our own ZK protocol is out: strategy §13 ranks custom cryptography a catastrophic-failure risk and Appendix A says prefer upstream reuse. | `contracts`, `infra`, `networks`, whitepaper, D9 audit scope | **Sequencing is not decided by this row.** testnet-1 first, mainnet only once there is enough baseline activity for a real anonymity set — a pool launched into thin volume gives its first users a guarantee that is sound in form and absent in practice (§10.1, threat model §4). Open before mainnet: who holds the association-set key (§10), and whether Cosmos's native ZK-UTXO module (H1 2026, with `x/poa`) supersedes the application-layer path. The pool is **not** allowlist-gated, preserving D6 semantics (D14). |
 
